- How does changing W affect S and latency? 
- How would you adapt for dynamic T or non-uniform bucket widths?

## Why This Is Asked

This problem tests real-time data tracking with batched updates and sliding window counts. It evaluates the ability to design a data structure that combines efficient timestamp-based aggregation with per-session lookup while managing amortized cleanup.

## Key Concepts

- Batched online updates with timestamp ordering
- Sliding window counts with expiration
- Two-tier data structures (ordered map + hash map)
- Amortized analysis versus naive recalculation

## Code Example

```javascript
class SessionTracker {
  constructor(W) {
    this.W = W;
    this.idToTime = new Map();
    this.timeToCount = new Map();
  }

  processBatch(events, currentTime) {
    for (const { sessionId, time, isNew } of events) {
      if (isNew) {
        this.idToTime.set(sessionId, time);
        this.timeToCount.set(time, (this.timeToCount.get(time) || 0) + 1);
      } else {
        const oldTime = this.idToTime.get(sessionId);
        this.timeToCount.set(oldTime, this.timeToCount.get(oldTime) - 1);
        this.idToTime.set(sessionId, time);
        this.timeToCount.set(time, (this.timeToCount.get(time) || 0) + 1);
      }
    }
    this.expireOldSessions(currentTime);
  }

  expireOldSessions(currentTime) {
    const threshold = currentTime - this.W;
    for (const [time, count] of this.timeToCount) {
      if (time < threshold) {
        this.timeToCount.delete(time);
      }
    }
  }

  countActive(currentTime) {
    const threshold = currentTime - this.W;
    let count = 0;
    for (const [time, c] of this.timeToCount) {
      if (time >= threshold) count += c;
    }
    return count;
  }
}
```

## Analysis

**Preprocessing**: O(1) — just initialize empty maps.

**Memory**: O(S) where S is the number of unique timestamps with active sessions; worst case O(N) for N sessions if all have distinct timestamps.

**Update**: Each event is O(log T) for the ordered map insertion/deletion, where T is the number of unique timestamps. With B events per batch, this is O(B log T) amortized.

**Query**: O(T) in the worst case — must scan all timestamps in the window. Using an ordered map with prefix sum augmentation could reduce this to O(log T).

**Comparison to naive recalculation**: Recalculating from scratch after each batch would require O(N) per query, where N is total active sessions. The two-tier structure provides O(1) lookup per session update and O(T) or O(log T) queries — a significant improvement when batches are processed frequently and queries are intermittent.

## Why This Is Asked

This question explores advanced incremental data-structure design under streaming constraints, requiring mastery of amortized analysis for batched updates alongside exact substring query capabilities.

## Key Concepts

- **Suffix Automaton fundamentals**: States, transitions, links, and the endpos equivalence relation
- **Batched extension strategies**: Buffering B characters before committing to the global structure
- **Clone management and amortized cost**: Deferred clone creation and lazy propagation techniques
- **Transition representations**: Compact maps vs. dense arrays for cache locality
- **Trade-off analysis**: Balancing batch size B, alphabet size σ, and total string length N

## Code Example

```cpp
struct State {
    int link;
    int len;
    std::unordered_map<char, int> next;  // or vector<int> for small σ
};

class BatchedSuffixAutomaton {
    std::vector<State> st;
    int last;
    std::string buffer;
    const size_t B;

public:
    BatchedSuffixAutomaton(size_t batch_size = 1024) : B(batch_size), last(0) {
        st.push_back({-1, 0, {}});
    }

    void extend(char c) {
        buffer.push_back(c);
        if (buffer.size() < B) return;
        flushBuffer();
    }

    void flushBuffer() {
        for (char c : buffer) {
            int cur = st.size();
            st.push_back({0, st[last].len + 1, {}});
            int p = last;
            while (p >= 0 && !st[p].next.count(c)) {
                st[p].next[c] = cur;
                p = st[p].link;
            }
            if (p == -1) {
                st[cur].link = 0;
            } else {
                int q = st[p].next[c];
                if (st[p].len + 1 == st[q].len) {
                    st[cur].link = q;
                } else {
                    int clone = st.size();
                    st.push_back(st[q]);  // clone
                    st[clone].len = st[p].len + 1;
                    while (p >= 0 && st[p].next[c] == q) {
                        st[p].next[c] = clone;
                        p = st[p].link;
                    }
                    st[q].link = st[cur].link = clone;
                }
            }
            last = cur;
        }
        buffer.clear();
    }

    bool query(const std::string& p) {
        int v = 0;
        for (char c : p) {
            if (!st[v].next.count(c)) return false;
            v = st[v].next[c];
        }
        return true;
    }
};
```

## Optimal Batch Size

The optimal B depends on workload characteristics:
- **Small σ (e.g., DNA, σ ≤ 4)**: Larger B (4096+) works well; dense transition arrays gain from cache locality
- **Large σ (e.g., Unicode, σ ≈ 10⁵)**: Smaller B (128–512); sparse maps reduce memory pressure
- **Memory-constrained environments**: B ≈ √N balances update frequency against per-batch overhead
- **Latency-sensitive queries**: Smaller B reduces worst-case query delay between flushes

